-- title:
--   Lemma 2.1 — functional versions; the copies are progressive (resp. continuous) and conditionally i.i.d. given $\mathcal F^0_t$
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a process of the mean field game, with values in a Euclidean space.
--
--   1. If $X$ is $\mathbb F$-progressive, there are measurable maps $\widetilde X_t$ on $\mathbb R^n\times\mathcal C_t\times\mathcal C_t$ such that
--   $$X_t=\widetilde X_t(\xi,W^0_{[0,t]},W_{[0,t]})\quad\text{a.s., for each }t\in[0,T],$$
--   and for each $N$ the processes $X^i_t:=\widetilde X_t(\xi^i,W^0_{[0,t]},W^i_{[0,t]})$, $i=1,\dots,N$, are $\mathbb F^N$-progressive and, for each fixed $t$, i.i.d. conditionally on $\mathcal F^0_t$.
--   2. If $X$ is continuous and $\mathbb F$-adapted, the same holds with the copies continuous and $\mathbb F^N$-adapted.
--
--   The process $\widetilde X_t(\xi,W^0_{[0,t]},W_{[0,t]})$ is the modification of the lemma, and the $X^i$ are the conditionally independent copies of Definition 2.2. They are the coupling between the mean field equilibrium and the $N$-player games.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the canonical spaces $\Omega^N=(\mathbb R^n)^N\times\mathcal C^{N+1}$ with product measure, embedded in one another (pp.9–10) → one probability space carrying $\xi^1,\xi^2,\dots$ (law $m_0$), $W^0$ and $W^1,W^2,\dots$, mutually independent, Brownian paths continuous (`Setup`); reason: the canonical product space is an instance, so the statement specializes to the paper's
--   - encoding: augmented filtrations $\mathbb F^N$, $\mathbb F^0$ → the raw filtrations generated by $\xi^1,\dots,\xi^N,W^0,\dots,W^N$ and by $W^0$; reason: every process progressive for the augmented filtration has a raw-progressive version ([41, Prop. 1.2.1], cited p.10); controls and states enter only up to versions
--   - encoding: continuous processes → processes with almost surely continuous paths on $[0,T]$; reason: on a general probability space continuity holds outside a null set; suprema and integrals do not see null sets
--   - reading: measurable maps $\widetilde X_t$ on $\mathbb R^d\times\mathcal C_t\times\mathcal C_t$ → on $\mathbb R^n\times\mathcal C_t\times\mathcal C_t$, with $\mathcal C_t$ represented by stopped paths $s\mapsto w(s\wedge t)$; reason: the initial state is $\mathbb R^n$-valued; a path on $[0,t]$ and its stopped extension determine each other
--   - encoding: "a modification of $X$ ... such that $X_t=\widetilde X_t(x,W^0_{[0,t]},W_{[0,t]})$ for each $t,x,\omega,\omega^0$" → the copy with index $1$, $\widetilde X_t(\xi,W^0_{[0,t]},W_{[0,t]})$, equals $X_t$ a.s. for each $t\le T$; its regularity is the $N=1$ case of the copies' regularity; reason: the modification is exactly that process
--   - encoding: "i.i.d. conditionally on $\mathcal F^0_t$" → conditional expectations of indicators: the conditional probability of the joint event factorizes, and all copies have the conditional law of the first; reason: definition of conditional independence and identical conditional distribution, without regular conditional kernels
--   - generalization: the processes of the paper ($\mathbb R^n$- or $\mathbb R^k$-valued) → processes with values in any $\mathbb R^p$; reason: the paper's processes are instances
--   - encoding: players $i\in\{1,\dots,N\}$ → players `i : Fin N`, player $i$ of the paper is index $i-1$; reason: relabelling
-- source:
--   Jackson & Tangpi, Quantitative Convergence for Displacement Monotone Mean Field Games with Controlled Volatility, arXiv:2304.04543v2, Lemma 2.1 and Definition 2.2, p.11

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CtrlVolMFG_IsSol_3_6

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace CtrlVolMFG

/-- Lemma 2.1 with Definition 2.2 (Jackson & Tangpi, arXiv:2304.04543v2, p.11). Let `X` be a
process of the mean field game (filtration `𝔽 = 𝔽¹`) with values in a Euclidean space.
1. If `X` is `𝔽`-progressive, there are measurable maps `X̃_t` on `ℝⁿ × 𝒞_t × 𝒞_t` such that
   `X_t = X̃_t(ξ, W⁰_{[0,t]}, W_{[0,t]})` almost surely for each `t ∈ [0, T]` (so the right side is a
   modification of `X`), and for every `N` the copies `Xⁱ_t = X̃_t(ξⁱ, W⁰_{[0,t]}, Wⁱ_{[0,t]})`,
   `i = 1, …, N`, are `𝔽ᴺ`-progressive (`ProgRep`; for `N = 1`, `i = 1` this is the modification)
   and i.i.d. conditionally on `ℱ⁰_t` for each `t`.
2. If `X` is `𝔽`-adapted with continuous paths on `[0, T]`, the same holds with the copies
   `𝔽ᴺ`-adapted with continuous paths (`ContRep`).

**Formalization Note** One probability space carries all players (`Setup`); "continuous" is
almost surely continuous on `[0, T]`; the modification is `X̃_t(ξ, W⁰_{[0,t]}, W_{[0,t]})` itself,
equal to `X_t` everywhere by construction. -/
theorem lemma_2_1 {n d p : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Setup n d Ω) (T : ℝ≥0)
    (X : ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin p)) :
    (IsStronglyProgressive (S.filtN 1) X → ∃ R : ProgRep S T X, R.toRep.CondIID) ∧
    (StronglyAdapted (S.filtN 1) X →
      (∀ᵐ ω ∂S.P, ContinuousOn (fun t => X t ω) (Set.Icc 0 T)) →
      ∃ R : ContRep S T X, R.toRep.CondIID) := by sorry

end CtrlVolMFG
